/inspirehep.net/files/971d7bf7906f2ed80d9a5c65d97dcfda","source":"arxiv","caption":"The momentum dependence of the particle production rate for the two-stage decay with an oscillating inflaton (model C). The function $p^2F(p)$has been plotted for $m_\\sigma=0$ at $T=0$ and $T=m_\\phi$.","filename":"figures_Sm.png"},{"key":"520a436507aed825ada77a46e6224e2e","url":"https://inspirehep.net/files/520a436507aed825ada77a46e6224e2e","source":"arxiv","caption":"The momentum dependence of the particle production rate for small $\\dot\\phi$ with the two-stage decay (model D). The function $p^2F(p)$is plotted. The thermal distribution $p^2n$ is shown for comparison.","filename":"figures_Sp.png"},{"key":"a72b3192704ffb02b2241124c10d0e89","url":"https://inspirehep.net/files/a72b3192704ffb02b2241124c10d0e89","source":"arxiv","caption":"The stationary momentum distribution for the oscillatory phase in the two-stage decay (model D) using the relaxation-time approximation. The relaxation times are $\\tau_r=0.1/H(0)$ (left) and $\\tau_r=0.05/H(0)$ (right). As might be expected, shorter relaxation times produce a spectrum which is closer to thermal equilibrium. The constant $R(0)=50H(0)$ and$m_\\phi=2H(0)$.","filename":"figures_fig4a.png"},{"key":"ca4811861328cb33ccae0d4cb004aa99","url":"https://inspirehep.net/files/ca4811861328cb33ccae0d4cb004aa99","source":"arxiv","caption":"The stationary momentum distribution for the oscillatory phase in the two-stage decay (model D) using the relaxation-time approximation. The relaxation times are $\\tau_r=0.1/H(0)$ (left) and $\\tau_r=0.05/H(0)$ (right). As might be expected, shorter relaxation times produce a spectrum which is closer to thermal equilibrium. The constant $R(0)=50H(0)$ and$m_\\phi=2H(0)$.","filename":"figures_fig4b.png"},{"key":"bf0c83b76a442e43fb7e9c2ad355f220","url":"https://inspirehep.net/files/bf0c83b76a442e43fb7e9c2ad355f220","source":"arxiv","caption":"The time evolution of the effective temperature for the oscillatory phase with the two-stage decay (model D) using the relaxation-time approximation. The de Sitter temperature $H/2\\pi$ is shown for comparison. The relaxation time$\\tau_r=0.1/H(0)$, the constant $R(0)=50H(0)$ and $m_\\phi=2H(0)$.","filename":"figures_fig5.png"},{"key":"0fe3be8ff5adef28889438fb11c5c711","url":"https://inspirehep.net/files/0fe3be8ff5adef28889438fb11c5c711","source":"arxiv","caption":"The stationary momentum distribution for different relaxation times in the two-stage decay (model D) using the relaxation-time approximation. The relaxation times are $\\tau_r=0.2/H$ (left) and $\\tau_r=0.05/H$ (right). As might be expected, shorter relaxation times produce a spectrum which is closer to thermal equilibrium. The constant $R=15H$ and$m_\\sigma=0.25H$.","filename":"figures_fig6a.png"},{"key":"3aa289b98a153e160cd1a971af53b1f1","url":"https://inspirehep.net/files/3aa289b98a153e160cd1a971af53b1f1","source":"arxiv","caption":"The stationary momentum distribution for different relaxation times in the two-stage decay (model D) using the relaxation-time approximation. The relaxation times are $\\tau_r=0.2/H$ (left) and $\\tau_r=0.05/H$ (right). As might be expected, shorter relaxation times produce a spectrum which is closer to thermal equilibrium. The constant $R=15H$ and$m_\\sigma=0.25H$.","filename":"figures_fig6b.png"},{"key":"762bb935e2ebb4ec26731f7cb280338f","url":"https://inspirehep.net/files/762bb935e2ebb4ec26731f7cb280338f","source":"arxiv","caption":"The time evolution of the effective temperature for different initial conditions with the two-stage decay (model D) using the relaxation-time approximation. In each case, the momentum distribution reaches a stationary state with the effective temperature shown. The relaxation time$\\tau_r=0.1/H$, the constant $R=15H$ and $m_\\sigma=0.25H$.","filename":"figures_fig7alt.png"},{"key":"e0e6ee8b233dc08362b7850f383bd5b2","url":"https://inspirehep.net/files/e0e6ee8b233dc08362b7850f383bd5b2","source":"arxiv","caption":"The stationary momentum distribution in the two-stage decay (model D) using the full collision integral gives a check for consistency of the relaxation time approximation used in Fig. \\ref{figbolz}. The parameters are $R=15H$ and $m_\\sigma=0.25H$ and $D=10$. The plot iscomparable to Fig. \\ref{figbolz} with a relaxation time $\\tau_r=0.1/H$.","filename":"figures_fig8.png"},{"key":"d069372fe4c97d4079f72e913581f3ac","url":"https://inspirehep.net/files/d069372fe4c97d4079f72e913581f3ac","source":"arxiv","caption":"The time evolution of the effective temperature for different initial conditions with the two-stage decay (model D) using the full collision integral. As with the relaxation time approximation, the momentum distribution reaches a stationary state with the effectivetemperature shown. The constant $D=10$, with $R=15H$ and $m_\\sigma=0.25H$.","filename":"figures_fig9.png"}],"texkeys":["Moss:2008lkw","Graham:2008vu"],"citeable":true,"keywords":[{"value":"98.80.Cq","schema":"PACS"},{"value":"particle: production","schema":"INSPIRE"},{"value":"inflaton: oscillation","schema":"INSPIRE"},{"value":"space-time: expansion","schema":"INSPIRE"},{"value":"inflation","schema":"INSPIRE"},{"value":"reheating","schema":"INSPIRE"},{"value":"field theory: finite temperature","schema":"INSPIRE"},{"value":"momentum dependence","schema":"INSPIRE"},{"value":"inflaton: decay","schema":"INSPIRE"},{"value":"Boltzmann equation","schema":"INSPIRE"},{"value":"numerical calculations","schema":"INSPIRE"}],"refereed":true,"abstracts":[{"value":"Thermal field theory is applied to particle production rates in inflationary models, leading to new results for catalysed, or two-stage decay, where massive fields act as decay channels for the production of light fields. A numerical investigation of the Bolztmann equation in an expanding universe shows that the particle distributions produced during small amplitude inflaton oscillations or alongside slowly moving inflaton fields can thermalise.","sour